Hi Mark,

We have investigated the reported defects in the Production and QC schemas. Please find our findings and updates for each issue below.

1. BBH Number and ISIN Columns – Blank Records (Production – RNA Mutual Fund Report)

Our investigation identified that the blank values were caused by the FULL OUTER JOIN introduced in the recent CR, which resulted in approximately 800 additional records compared to the existing MS Access output.

We have addressed this by incorporating COALESCE logic to retrieve the BBH Number (Security ID) and ISIN/Stock Code from the child records (Monthly Hedge Views) whenever the corresponding values are unavailable in the parent records (Master Position Views).

The updated logic now populates these fields correctly, and the issue is resolved.

2. BBH Account Number Displaying as OMNIBUS (Production – RNA Mutual Fund Report)

This issue was already addressed in the previous deployment. The reported defects appear to have been identified before that deployment.

However, during our investigation, we found a few records where OMNIBUS was still displayed because STRING_14 was empty in both the parent (Master Position) and child (Monthly Hedge) records. Additionally, the LOCAL_ACC_NO did not contain a hyphen, which is required by the existing account-number extraction logic.

When we executed the report yesterday, five such records were identified. However, in today's execution, no OMNIBUS records were found. Therefore, the issue is currently resolved in the latest report output.

3. BBH Account Name – Blank Records (Production – RNA Mutual Fund Report)

We investigated the BBH Account Name population logic and found that our BIRT report already populates several BBH Account Names that are blank in the original MS Access output.

This is achieved through an additional grouping-based logic using the BBH Account Number. Where a BBH Account Name is available for a particular BBH Account Number within the Master Position records, the same account name is used to populate other records with the matching BBH Account Number where the account name is blank.

However, for the remaining records reported as blank, our investigation found that STRING_29 (BBH Account Name) is empty in both the parent (Master Position Views) and child (Monthly Hedge Views) records.

Since no BBH Account Name is available in either source record, the existing grouping logic cannot retrieve or populate the account name for these cases.

Therefore, the BIRT report is already applying the available fallback logic, and the remaining blank values are due to the absence of BBH Account Name data in both underlying views.

4. AIFOP Mutual Fund – BBH External Account Number (QC Schema)

We investigated the reported issue for ISIN 34YX M5.

Our investigation identified five corresponding records in the Monthly Hedge Views (child records). For all five Item IDs, STRING_21 contains the external account number.

We also verified the corresponding records in the Master Position Break Views (parent records) using the Stock Code and Local Account Number combinations. The corresponding records were present there as well, and STRING_21 was populated for all five records.

As per the agreed logic shared in David's Excel, the report retrieves the external account number from the Master Position Views (parent records), applying the Ledger-first and Statement-second prioritisation logic to select the appropriate record.

We also reviewed the TLM screenshot shared in the defect. The External Account Number field appears to contain values in the screenshot.

Based on these findings, we could identify the external account numbers in both the parent and child records. Therefore, we could not reproduce the reported scenario where TLM does not contain an external account number.

Could you please share the specific Item IDs investigated on your side and clarify the exact TLM selection criteria used to identify the missing external account number? This will help us investigate the discrepancy further.

5. Record Count Validation – MS Access vs. Oracle SQL (Production)

As part of the investigation, we also compared the production MS Access output with the Oracle SQL output.

MS Access output: 3,519 records.

Oracle SQL with INNER JOIN: 3,519 records.

Oracle SQL with FULL OUTER JOIN: 4,320 records.

This confirms that the FULL OUTER JOIN introduced approximately 800 additional records, which contributed to the blank BBH Number, ISIN and BBH Account Name observations.

We have addressed the BBH Number and ISIN population for these additional records through the updated child-record fallback logic.

